      ],
      "name": "Purpose & Authority",
      "description": "This Personnel Management Regulations Manual is prescribed for the purpose of carrying out the personnel program pursuant to Chapter 2.56 of the Marin County Code known as the Merit System Ordinance, and compliance with State and federal law. PMR 1 establishes the purpose and authority for these regulations.",
      "citation": [
        {
          "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1"
        }
      ],
      "legislationDateOfApplicability": "2018-09-06",
      "legislationDateVersion": "2020-10-13",
      "version": "2",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "local:pmr-prepared-by-as-written",
          "value": "Mary Hao"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "local:pmr-approved-by-as-written",
          "value": "ss/President, Board of Supervisors"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1/1.1",
      "@type": "Legislation",
      "legislationIdentifier": "1.1",
      "name": "Purpose",
      "codificationLevel": "section",
      "position": 1,
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1"
      },
      "text": "A. The goal of these regulations is to establish equitable and lawful procedures for dealing with personnel matters, to attract and retain for the County service the most competent persons available, to assure that appointments and promotions of employees will be based on merit and fitness, to provide a reasonable degree of security for qualified employees, and to support the values, goals, and strategies set forth in the County’s Strategic Plan.\n\nB. These PMRs represent a compilation of legal standards, policies, procedures, rules, forms, and definitions pertaining to the responsibilities of employees and managers in their employment with the County of Marin. They incorporate previous Personnel Commission Rules and Regulations, the County Code (Section 2.52 [Salary], and Section 2.56 [Merit System]), and numerous other countywide policies and procedures. These previous personnel rules, regulations, policies, and procedures are expressly repealed and replaced by these PMRs.\n\nC. The County's personnel program, including but not limited to the Merit System, all policies, procedures and practices, will be aimed at excellence and merit. It will encourage competence, accomplishment, learning, diversity, equity, purpose and loyalty in the public service.\n\nD. These Personnel Management Regulations are organized into the following sections:\n\n- Section 1: General and Administration\n- Section 2: Personnel Conduct and Standards of Employment\n- Section 3: Recruitment and Selection\n- Section 4: Employment Practices\n\nThese policies and procedures apply to all employees of the County of Marin unless a specific policy or procedure indicates otherwise. The provisions related to administration of a Merit System, e.g. appointment, probation status, procedure for disciplinary action and selection appeals, are not applicable to\n\n- Elective officials;\n- Appointed department heads except where otherwise provided by law;\n- Members of appointive boards, commissions and committees;\n- Positions established for the purpose of a special study or investigation requiring expert professional or technical services for a definite period of time;\n- Patients or inmates of county institutions;\n- Participants in the Adult Offender Work Program;\n- One chief deputy or assistant of each elective official;\n- Assistant County Executive;\n- Assistant Director of Finance;\n- Assistant Director of Parks & Open Space;\n- Assistant Public Defender;\n- Chief Fiscal Officer – Health & Human Services;\n- Chief Information Security Officer;\n- Deputy Director of County Library Services;\n- Deputy Director of Human Resources;\n- Deputy Fire Chief - Administration;\n- Administrative aides appointed by each member of the Board of Supervisors, not to exceed two full-time positions or the equivalent thereof;\n- Appointees on or after December 13, 2016 in Chief Assistant Director(s) and Assistant Director(s) classifications in the following departments: Agriculture, Weights and Measures; Assessor-Recorder County Clerk; Child Support Services; Community Development Agency; County Counsel; Cultural Services; Elections; Farm; Fire; Health and Human Services; Human Resources; Information Services and Technology; Library; Probation; Public Works; and Retirement;\n- Appointees on or after November 12, 2020 in the following classifications: Deputy County Executive, Deputy Director of Environmental Health Services, Deputy Director of Building Inspection and Safety, and Deputy Fire Chief;\n- Appointees on or after May 13, 2021 in the classification of Equity Director; and\n- Appointees on or after August 19, 2021 in the classification of Deputy Director of Housing & Federal Grants.\n- Appointees on or after October 20, 2022 in the classification of Director of Emergency Management.\n- Appointees on or after October 20, 2022 in the classification of Deputy Director of Planning Services.\n- Appointees on or after December 8, 2022 in the classification of Budget Director.\n- Appointees on or after August 18, 2023 in the classification of Employee & Labor Relations Director.\n- Appointees on or after June 21, 2024 in the classification of Legislative & Intergovernmental Affairs Director.\n- Appointees on or after January 16, 2024 in the classification of Inspector General.\n- Appointees on or after November 4, 2025 in the classification of Sustainability & Climate Adaptation Director.\n- Appointees on or after May 10, 2026 in the classification of Flood Control & Water Conservation District General Manager.\n- Appointees on or after July 7, 2026 in the classification of Economic Vitality & Municipal Services Director.",
      "legislationType": "regulation"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1/1.3",
      "@type": "Legislation",
      "legislationIdentifier": "1.3",
      "name": "Authority",
      "codificationLevel": "section",
      "position": 2,
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1"
      },
      "text": "A. The ultimate determination of personnel policy is the responsibility of the Board of Supervisors, after development by the Director of Human Resources and County Administrator, and review and recommendation by the Personnel Commission.\n\nB. The Board of Supervisors may establish the position of Director of Human Resources to provide for the conducting of competitive examinations to ascertain the fitness of applicants for appointment and for the promotion of employees to vacant positions, and in conjunction with the County Administrator, to have the responsibilities to administer all aspects of the personnel system. The Director of Human Resources may contract with any qualified person or agency for the performance of such technical or professional services as may be desired in the establishment or operation of the personnel system. Administration includes keeping the Personnel Commission informed of developments, issues, and anticipated developments.\n\nC. The Director of Human Resources, or designee, acting as Executive Secretary to the Personnel Commission, is authorized to execute Personnel Commission policy and the establishment of such administrative controls as may be necessary to effectuate the provisions of these Personnel Management Regulations.",
      "legislationType": "regulation"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1/1.4",
      "@type": "Legislation",
      "legislationIdentifier": "1.4",
      "name": "Effect of Collective Bargaining Agreements",
      "codificationLevel": "section",
      "position": 3,
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1"
      },
      "text": "If a provision of these Personnel Management Regulations is in conflict with a provision of an applicable collective bargaining agreement negotiated between the County and a recognized employee organization, to the extent of such conflict, the provision of the collective bargaining agreement shall be controlling.",
      "legislationType": "regulation"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1/1.5",
      "@type": "Legislation",
      "legislationIdentifier": "1.5",
      "name": "No Contract Created",
      "codificationLevel": "section",
      "position": 4,
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1"
      },
      "text": "The Personnel Management Regulations do not create any contract of employment, express or implied, or any rights in the nature of a contract.",
      "legislationType": "regulation"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1/1.6",
      "@type": "Legislation",
      "legislationIdentifier": "1.6",
      "name": "Right to Amend, Delete or Suspend",
      "codificationLevel": "section",
      "position": 5,
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-1"
      },
      "text": "These policies may be amended by adding, deleting or changing such policies from time to time upon giving at least one calendar week written notice to each of the bargaining units of the County of Marin. Such notice that an amendment to a particular policy or policies is to be considered will provide an opportunity for a meeting, the time and place of which is specified in the notice. The bargaining units will have one calendar week from receipt of the notice to respond to the notice and opportunity provided. This section is not intended to supercede the meet and confer obligations set forth in the California Government Code Section 3500 et. seq.",
      "legislationType": "regulation"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-2",
      "@type": "Legislation",
      "legislationIdentifier": "PMR 2",
      "codificationLevel": "chapter",
      "legislationType": "regulation",
      "position": 2,
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/personnel-management-regulations/part-1"
      },
      "hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-2/2.1"
        }
      ],
      "name": "Definitions",
      "description": "This Personnel Management Regulation (PMR) defines words and terminology used by these County of Marin PMRs.",
      "version": "0",
      "identifier": [
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "local:pmr-effective-date-as-written",
          "value": "2005-01"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "local:pmr-prepared-by-as-written",
          "value": "Laura Armor"
        },
        {
          "@type": "PropertyValue",
          "propertyID": "local:pmr-approved-by-as-written",
          "value": "ss/President, Board of Supervisors"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-2/2.1",
      "@type": "Legislation",
      "legislationIdentifier": "2.1",
      "name": "Definitions",
      "codificationLevel": "section",
      "position": 1,
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-2"
      },
      "text": "Unless otherwise defined by the context, words used in these PMRs will have the following meanings:\n\nA. Administrator. The County Administrator or his or her designee.\n\nB. Allocation. The assignment of a position to an appropriate classification as a budgetary action by the Board of Supervisors.\n\nC. Anniversary Date. The date recurring yearly upon an employee's most recent regular appointment.\n\nD. Applicant. Any person who, according to these rules, has made formal application for employment.\n\nE. Appointing Authority. The person(s) having authority to appoint or to remove persons from positions in the County service or subordinates to whom this authority is delegated.\n\nF. Appointment. The offer of and acceptance by a candidate to a position in the county service. Types of appointments are described in PMR 34.2.\n\nG. Board. The Board of Supervisors of the County of Marin.\n\nH. Certification. The submission of names of eligible candidates from an employment list to an appointing authority by the Director of Human Resources or his or her designee.\n\nI. Classification or Class. A position or group of positions having the same title, class specification, minimum qualifications and salary.\n\nJ. Classification Plan. The arrangement of positions into classes together with specifications describing each classification, approved by the Board of Supervisors.\n\nK. Commission. The Personnel Commission of the County of Marin.\n\nL. County. The County of Marin government organization and all special districts governed solely by the Board of Supervisors, e.g. Open Space District.\n\nM. Day. Calendar day. If the final day of a time period falls on a weekend or holiday, the next calendar day following will constitute the final calendar day.\n\nN. Demotion. A change in status of an employee from a position in one classification to a position in another classification with lesser duties and responsibilities, lower qualifications, and a lower maximum salary. A demotion may be voluntary or involuntary.\n\nO. Eligible Candidate. A person who has successfully passed all initial examination requirements for a classification for which he/she has made official application and has been placed on an eligible list.\n\nP. Eligible List. An officially promulgated list of eligible candidates for a specific classification in rank order of final examination score, re-employment rights, or transfer.\n\nQ. Employee. A person who is employed by the County.\n\nR. Exempt Employee. An employee defined under federal law as not subject to overtime payment over 40 hours per week or the equivalent. Also known under federal law as a salaried employee.\n\nS. Merit System. The system of personnel administration governing all positions and/or classifications and the employees occupying such positions, except those specifically exempted in Section 2.56.040 of the County Code, as set forth in PMR 1.2. In addition, at-will, temporary, emergency, or provisional appointment positions are not included in the Merit System.\n\nT. Non-Exempt Employee. An employee who under federal law must be paid premium wages (or compensatory time off) when he or she works over 40 hours per week or the equivalent. Also known under federal law as an hourly employee.\n\nU. Overtime. Authorized work beyond the identified week or work period.\n\nV. Probationary Period. A trial period during which an employee in the Merit System is required to demonstrate competency in the knowledge, skills and abilities necessary to successfully perform the job and from which an employee may be released without cause or right of appeal. The probationary period is the final step in the examination process.\n\nW. Promotion. Advancement of an employee from a position in an established classification to a position in an established classification with higher level duties and responsibilities, higher qualifications and a higher maximum salary.\n\nX. Reassignment. A move of an employee from one division or budget center to another within the same department in the same or similar classification at the same rate of pay.\n\nY. Reclassification. A reallocation of a position and the incumbent, if applicable, to a different or new classification because of a significant change in duties and/or responsibility of the position over time.\n\nZ. Reinstatement. A return to employment of an employee following leave without pay, upon reemployment following layoff, or return within 60 days of resignation.\n\nAA. Reorganization: A planned, prospective restructuring or redesign of a department, which will have fiscal or classification impact, to meet changing program needs.\n\nAB. Secretary to the Personnel Commission: The Director of Human Resources is designated as Executive Secretary to the Personnel Commission.\n\nAC. Separation. Any ending of employment with the County, including discharge, reduction in force, resignation, retirement, and job abandonment.\n\nAD. Sliding Classifications. Classifications within a series specifically designated as sliding classifications by Human Resources where a) the work is normally performed at the higher level; b) positions in the class series appointed at a lower level are expected, and in some cases required, to advance to a higher level once the employee meets the requirements of the higher level and is functioning effectively at that level; and c) the employee has passed probation in the lower level class.\n\nAE. Step Increase. Advancement within a salary range from one step to another, higher step.\n\nAF. Step Increase Date. Date used to determine eligibility for a step increase.\n\nAG. Termination. The involuntary separation from employment of an employee by an appointing authority. Dismissal and discharge may be used synonymously with termination.\n\nAH. Transfer. A move of an employee who meets the minimum qualifications from one department to another either in the same classification or another classification at the same salary level. This is in contrast to reassignment within the same department.\n\nAI. Transfer List. List of eligible employees with expressed interest in being considered for transfer.\n\nAJ. Y-Rate. A salary paid above the maximum salary of the range for an incumbent employee when the employee is moved from his or her class to a different class with a lower salary range. The incumbent retains his or her current rate of pay until such time as the new class has a maximum salary rate which is equal to or higher than the \"y\" rate.",
      "citation": [
        {
          "@id": "https://marincountygov.github.io/marin-data/id/regulation/pmr-34/34.2"
        },
        "PMR 1.2"
      ],
      "legislationType": "regulation"
    },
